course: dictionary
lesson: 2589
title: Dental: all about teeth
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: "Dental" is an adjective, a describing word. It tells you that something is about teeth or about looking after teeth. Read the words around it to check: «Use dental floss to clean between your teeth» tells you that dental floss is something for cleaning teeth.
explain: «Never use a fork, pin or other sharp object, which can cut the gums and chip teeth.» The sentence tells you what harms teeth, so you can work out that dental care means looking after your teeth properly.
explain: Dental often comes before another word, as in a dental check-up, dental care, a dental nurse or dental hygiene. Try the swap test: put "teeth" in place of "dental" and see if it still makes sense. A teeth check-up is a check-up of your teeth, so the meaning fits.
fact: Dental means about teeth or about looking after teeth.
fact: "Dental" is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like dental floss or dental check-up.
fact: Dental hygiene means keeping your teeth clean, such as brushing them twice a day.
fact: Use dental floss to clean between your teeth. Never use a fork, a pin or any sharp object, because sharp things can cut the gums and chip teeth.
exercise: In «dental floss», what does dental mean? | About bones | *About teeth | About hair | About ears
exercise: Which should you never use to clean between your teeth? | A clean cloth | *A sharp pin | Dental floss | Warm water
exercise: Why can a sharp object be dangerous for your teeth? | Because it is good for dental hygiene | Because it tastes nice | Because it makes teeth shine | *Because it can cut the gums and chip teeth
test: A dental check-up is a visit to | the football pitch | the library | *the dentist to look after your teeth
test: Which phrase names the thing you use to clean between your teeth? | dental nurse | dental check-up | *dental floss
exam: Brushing your teeth twice a day is an example of | a sharp object | *dental hygiene | a dental nurse | a cut gum
